WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:00.973
- It seemed

00:00:00.973 --> 00:00:03.200
that nothing could touch New
York governor Andrew Cuomo

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.030
after helping the state battle back

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:06.898
against its high COVID 19 infection rates.

00:00:06.898 --> 00:00:08.475
He even wrote a book about it

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:11.559
but then governor Cuomo had
to face allegations related

00:00:11.559 --> 00:00:13.023
to two separate scandals.

00:00:13.023 --> 00:00:15.958
Each of which might have been
enough to topple a governor

00:00:15.958 --> 00:00:19.105
who wasn't receiving such
a stellar approval ratings.

00:00:19.105 --> 00:00:21.644
At this point, multiple
people have come forward

00:00:21.644 --> 00:00:24.470
accusing governor Cuomo of multiple acts

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:26.825
of sexual harassment, and
many are accusing the governor

00:00:26.825 --> 00:00:29.071
of having perpetrated
a policy that has led

00:00:29.071 --> 00:00:31.838
to the deaths of thousands
of nursing home residents.

00:00:31.838 --> 00:00:34.777
What are these allegations
and is governor Cuomo

00:00:34.777 --> 00:00:35.795
in legal hot water.

00:00:35.795 --> 00:00:36.778
(upbeat music)

00:00:36.778 --> 00:00:39.766
Sponsored by ting mobile.

00:00:39.766 --> 00:00:42.770
So the first scandal plaguing Andrew Cuomo

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:43.838
involves sexual harassment.

00:00:43.838 --> 00:00:46.440
So far seven women have
accused the governor

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.147
of sexual harassment of various kinds.

00:00:48.147 --> 00:00:50.420
And in response, Cuomo has dished

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:52.576
out the sexual harassment
defense combo played it

00:00:52.576 --> 00:00:55.071
heaping tablespoon,
invited, and do it aside

00:00:55.071 --> 00:00:57.467
of cancel culture and an extra helping of

00:00:57.467 --> 00:00:59.527
"I know now the times have changed

00:00:59.527 --> 00:01:01.333
"and these interactions
are no longer appropriate."

00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:03.722
- I understand that
sensitivities have changed

00:01:03.722 --> 00:01:08.722
and behavior has changed and I get it.

00:01:09.758 --> 00:01:10.924
And I'm gonna learn from it.

00:01:10.924 --> 00:01:14.204
- Governor, it's 2021, sexual
harassment has been an issue

00:01:14.204 --> 00:01:15.599
for a very long time.

00:01:15.599 --> 00:01:17.320
And you're claiming
that you didn't realize

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:19.249
that times have changed until this year.

00:01:19.249 --> 00:01:22.748
That in and of itself is a
problem, especially because less

00:01:22.748 --> 00:01:25.810
than two years ago, Cuomo
criticized the legal standard

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:28.102
for sexual harassment in
a highly informed way.

00:01:28.102 --> 00:01:30.583
- The current law says sexual harassment

00:01:30.583 --> 00:01:35.448
has to be severe or pervasive.

00:01:35.448 --> 00:01:37.414
Severe or pervasive.

00:01:37.414 --> 00:01:42.414
Look at those words, it has
to be severe to break the law.

00:01:44.686 --> 00:01:46.494
It has to be pervasive.

00:01:46.494 --> 00:01:51.129
And when you look at the
case law on that threshold

00:01:51.129 --> 00:01:56.091
you have some despicable acts

00:01:56.091 --> 00:01:59.557
that have been performed repeatedly,

00:01:59.557 --> 00:02:02.032
where the court has determined.

00:02:02.032 --> 00:02:07.032
They're not severe enough, or
they're not pervasive enough.

00:02:07.265 --> 00:02:10.529
"They are only sporadic".The court said.

00:02:10.529 --> 00:02:14.650
And sporadic is not pervasive.

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:19.650
Oh, so I can sporadically
sexually harass someone as long

00:02:21.195 --> 00:02:25.442
as I don't go so far as
it becomes pervasive.

00:02:25.442 --> 00:02:28.494
- So it sounds like governor
Cuomo was very familiar

00:02:28.494 --> 00:02:30.171
with modern sexual harassment law.

00:02:30.171 --> 00:02:32.653
But since he's now very curious
about the state of the law

00:02:32.653 --> 00:02:35.156
what is the legal standard
for sexual harassment?

00:02:35.156 --> 00:02:38.109
And what is the distinction
between acts that are sporadic

00:02:38.109 --> 00:02:40.427
and behavior that is legally pervasive?

00:02:40.427 --> 00:02:42.462
Well, we start with the
definition of sexual harassment.

00:02:42.462 --> 00:02:43.868
Sexual harassment is a form

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:45.978
of sex discrimination
that violates title seven

00:02:45.978 --> 00:02:47.090
of the civil rights act of 1964.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:50.360
We generally use the
term sexual harassment to

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:53.410
describe a wide range of
workplace conduct, but

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:55.500
for sexual harassment to
actually be actionable in court

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.421
it must satisfy a legal standard.

00:02:57.421 --> 00:03:00.458
Sexual harassment can consist
of unwanted sexual advances

00:03:00.458 --> 00:03:03.180
or other unwelcome
conduct that is motivated

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.013
by the victim's gender.

00:03:04.013 --> 00:03:07.030
This conduct can result in
a tangible personal action

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:09.510
like a demotion, or is
so severe or pervasive

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:12.273
as to alter the terms and
conditions of employment.

00:03:12.273 --> 00:03:14.657
Now who can be a victim
of sexual discrimination.

00:03:14.657 --> 00:03:17.030
Really anyone the perpetrator may be

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:18.204
of the same or opposite sex

00:03:18.204 --> 00:03:21.662
or maybe a supervisor or a
coworker, or even a non-employee.

00:03:21.662 --> 00:03:24.960
And when the harasser is the
boss courts can generally

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.499
perceive the situation to be more severe.

00:03:26.499 --> 00:03:29.208
Now, there are generally two
kinds of sexual harassment.

00:03:29.208 --> 00:03:31.010
The first type is often referred

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.371
to as quid pro quo, harassment

00:03:32.371 --> 00:03:34.800
quid pro quo harassment
occurs when a person

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.730
most often the supervisor
asks an employee to

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:39.008
perform some sort of sexual favor

00:03:39.008 --> 00:03:41.996
in exchange for benefits
like a raise or promotion.

00:03:41.996 --> 00:03:44.950
And this is also a threat
scenario where a supervisor

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:46.856
pressures a subordinate to
do something that they want

00:03:46.856 --> 00:03:49.554
or suffer an adverse
consequence like termination

00:03:49.554 --> 00:03:53.206
or provide some benefit
for taking the action

00:03:53.206 --> 00:03:54.559
that they wouldn't otherwise take.

00:03:54.559 --> 00:03:56.120
Now, the second type

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.346
of sexual harassment is called
a hostile work environment.

00:03:58.346 --> 00:04:00.312
And a hostile work environment is one

00:04:00.312 --> 00:04:03.363
in which "discriminatory
intimidation, ridicule

00:04:03.363 --> 00:04:05.973
and insult is sufficiently
severe or pervasive

00:04:05.973 --> 00:04:08.682
as to alter the conditions
of a victim's employment."

00:04:08.682 --> 00:04:10.390
So let's talk about that severe

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:13.217
and pervasive standard in
Harris V. Forklift systems.

00:04:13.217 --> 00:04:15.070
The Supreme court said that determining

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:16.767
whether a workplace is sufficiently toxic

00:04:16.767 --> 00:04:19.156
"is not, and by its nature cannot

00:04:19.156 --> 00:04:21.299
"be a mathematically precise test."

00:04:21.299 --> 00:04:23.610
And the court set forth
a number of factors

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:25.300
that courts have to
consider when evaluating

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.590
whether conduct is severe
and pervasive enough

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:28.925
to create a hostile work environment

00:04:28.925 --> 00:04:31.860
such as how frequent is
the discriminatory conduct.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.129
How severe is it?

00:04:33.129 --> 00:04:34.308
Is it physically threatening?

00:04:34.308 --> 00:04:35.529
Is the conduct humiliating?

00:04:35.529 --> 00:04:37.440
Does the behavior interfere

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:38.363
with an employee's work performance?

00:04:38.363 --> 00:04:40.094
Is the employee complaining

00:04:40.094 --> 00:04:41.456
of isolated comments?

00:04:41.456 --> 00:04:43.853
And this isn't a mathematical test.

00:04:43.853 --> 00:04:46.160
There is a true controversy
over the ways courts

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:47.781
apply title seven severe
and pervasive standard.

00:04:47.781 --> 00:04:52.781
- Oh. So I can sporadically
sexually harass someone as long

00:04:53.763 --> 00:04:58.057
as I don't go so far as
it becomes pervasive.

00:04:58.057 --> 00:05:00.204
- Well, yeah, that is possible.

00:05:00.204 --> 00:05:01.325
So let's talk about

00:05:01.325 --> 00:05:04.083
the allegations against
governor Cuomo himself.

00:05:04.083 --> 00:05:06.760
Cuomo had a lot to say about how the legal

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:08.079
standard hurts sexual harassment victims

00:05:08.079 --> 00:05:10.580
but what do the women who worked

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:12.821
for Cuomo have to say about
his particular conduct?

00:05:12.821 --> 00:05:15.550
Charlotte Bennett, a
25 year old former aid

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:17.390
to the governor said he
hit on her several times.

00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:18.378
He asked her questions, including

00:05:18.378 --> 00:05:19.667
whether she had,

00:05:19.667 --> 00:05:20.901
"Ever been with an older man?"

00:05:20.901 --> 00:05:23.348
and whether she was monogamous
in her relationships

00:05:23.348 --> 00:05:26.050
Cuomo knew Bennett had been
the victim of sexual abuse

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:27.161
and once asked her if she,

00:05:27.161 --> 00:05:29.777
"Had trouble enjoying being with someone

00:05:29.777 --> 00:05:31.016
"because of her trauma."

00:05:31.016 --> 00:05:34.100
Cuomo didn't dispute Bennet's
allegations explaining instead

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.084
that he had never
intended to offend anyone.

00:05:36.084 --> 00:05:38.897
"At work sometimes I
think I am being playful

00:05:38.897 --> 00:05:40.577
"and make jokes that I think are funny."

00:05:40.577 --> 00:05:41.820
"I do on occasion, tease people

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:44.382
"in what I think is a good natured way."

00:05:44.382 --> 00:05:46.583
But let's use some common sense.

00:05:46.583 --> 00:05:49.160
If the boss asked
somebody how their history

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:50.280
of abuse impacts their sex life

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:52.777
does that really truly fit the,

00:05:52.777 --> 00:05:54.053
"It was just a joke defense."

00:05:54.053 --> 00:05:57.564
It depends on how a jury
would these circumstances

00:05:57.564 --> 00:05:59.665
but it doesn't seem like
a jokey conversation.

00:05:59.665 --> 00:06:02.288
Or at least not one
that should be tolerated

00:06:02.288 --> 00:06:03.132
in this day and age.

00:06:03.132 --> 00:06:04.690
Now the Supreme court's severe

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:06.357
and pervasive standard was meant to,

00:06:06.357 --> 00:06:07.576
"Filter out complaints attacking

00:06:07.576 --> 00:06:10.707
"the ordinary tribulations
of the workplace

00:06:10.707 --> 00:06:13.092
"such as the sporadic
use of abuse of language

00:06:13.092 --> 00:06:16.038
"gender related jokes,
and occasional teasing."

00:06:16.038 --> 00:06:19.700
A few straight remarks often
may make work uncomfortable

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:21.180
but they don't automatically make the work

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:22.651
environment hostile in the legal sense.

00:06:22.651 --> 00:06:25.735
But the other allegations
against Cuomo go even further.

00:06:25.735 --> 00:06:28.435
Other victims say that
Cuomo was not just a talker.

00:06:28.435 --> 00:06:29.931
He was also a toucher.

00:06:29.931 --> 00:06:32.370
An anonymous former aide says

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:35.072
that Cuomo groped her Cuomo
allegedly called the woman

00:06:35.072 --> 00:06:37.070
to the governor's mansion under the guise

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:38.396
of needing help with his cell phone.

00:06:38.396 --> 00:06:40.084
According to a police report,

00:06:40.084 --> 00:06:42.792
"They were alone in
Cuomo's private residence

00:06:42.792 --> 00:06:44.096
"on the second floor of the mansion

00:06:44.096 --> 00:06:46.837
"When he closed the door
and allegedly reached

00:06:46.837 --> 00:06:48.671
"under her blouse and
began to fondle her."

00:06:48.671 --> 00:06:49.805
The woman came forward

00:06:49.805 --> 00:06:52.263
after the people noticed
that she was crying

00:06:52.263 --> 00:06:54.484
while watching Cuomo
deny other allegations

00:06:54.484 --> 00:06:55.943
of sexual harassment against him.

00:06:55.943 --> 00:06:58.720
Another former Cuomo
aid Lindsey Boylan said

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:00.650
that Cuomo once kissed
her without her consent

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:02.214
and suggested they play strip poker.

00:07:02.214 --> 00:07:04.961
Boylan says that Cuomo told
her he had a crush on her

00:07:04.961 --> 00:07:07.207
and started calling her by
the name of his ex-girlfriend.

00:07:07.207 --> 00:07:09.611
And apparently his actions
may have escalated over.

00:07:09.611 --> 00:07:13.636
- I am embarrassed by it.

00:07:13.636 --> 00:07:15.514
- Another woman allegedly
had the same experience

00:07:15.514 --> 00:07:16.727
as Boylan.

00:07:16.727 --> 00:07:19.142
Another former Cuomo staffer analysts

00:07:19.142 --> 00:07:21.590
told the wall street journal
that Cuomo started out

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:23.110
by asking her about her love life

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:24.333
and calling her sweetheart

00:07:24.333 --> 00:07:27.419
which escalated to touching
her back, kissing her hand.

00:07:27.419 --> 00:07:29.643
Eventually he called her Sparky, Blondie

00:07:29.643 --> 00:07:30.861
sweetheart and honey.

00:07:30.861 --> 00:07:33.250
Now, when you combine unwanted touching

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:35.812
and unwanted advances and
comments, you're probably

00:07:35.812 --> 00:07:38.255
in the ballpark of a
legally actionable claim

00:07:38.255 --> 00:07:40.534
for a hostile work
environment under title seven.

00:07:40.534 --> 00:07:43.154
Now, presumably Cuomo will still claim

00:07:43.154 --> 00:07:44.823
that these were allegations
from different people

00:07:44.823 --> 00:07:48.111
at different times, and no
one filed a formal complaint

00:07:48.111 --> 00:07:50.742
against him and all of these
things even taken together

00:07:50.742 --> 00:07:53.316
don't rise to the severe
and pervasive standard

00:07:53.316 --> 00:07:56.209
to make a hostile work environment claim

00:07:56.209 --> 00:07:57.758
under sexual harassment law.

00:07:57.758 --> 00:08:00.130
And some of these things are in fact

00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:02.195
valid defenses under title seven,

00:08:02.195 --> 00:08:05.117
but perhaps not all of it.

00:08:05.117 --> 00:08:07.910
Which takes us to the
New York Human Rights Act

00:08:07.910 --> 00:08:09.900
because title seven is a federal law

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:12.040
and it's not the only law that's at issue

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:13.268
when you're talking
about sexual harassment.

00:08:13.268 --> 00:08:16.836
What about New York law in
what may be a delicious irony.

00:08:16.836 --> 00:08:18.281
Andrew Cuomo is actually responsible

00:08:18.281 --> 00:08:20.760
for changing the way
that New York interprets

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:22.650
the severe and pervasive standard,

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:24.270
which should actually make it easier

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:25.695
for his potential victims to sue.

00:08:25.695 --> 00:08:28.245
New York was actually one of
the first states to decide

00:08:28.245 --> 00:08:30.770
that the severe and
pervasive standard let too

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:33.362
many people get away with
offensive workplace behavior.

00:08:33.362 --> 00:08:36.840
Previously New York state
law, mirrored title seven

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:38.611
and required an employee to show

00:08:38.611 --> 00:08:40.960
that the alleged harassment
was severe and pervasive

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:43.933
in order to say an actionable
claim against an employer.

00:08:43.933 --> 00:08:47.529
But in 2019 New York
amended its human rights

00:08:47.529 --> 00:08:49.183
act to eliminate the severe

00:08:49.183 --> 00:08:51.513
and pervasive standard
entirely under the act.

00:08:51.513 --> 00:08:53.670
And employee only has to show

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:55.261
that the alleged harassment or rises

00:08:55.261 --> 00:08:59.802
above the level of petty slights
and trivial inconveniences.

00:08:59.802 --> 00:09:03.610
The new law also removes
one of the key defenses used

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:05.634
by employers that the employee never used.

00:09:05.634 --> 00:09:08.282
The employer's internal
complaint procedure.

00:09:08.282 --> 00:09:11.519
There's also a new three
year statute of limitations.

00:09:11.519 --> 00:09:14.230
So if Cuomo did indeed harass anyone

00:09:14.230 --> 00:09:15.439
over the last three years

00:09:15.439 --> 00:09:18.207
they have a clear path to
Sue under New York law.

00:09:18.207 --> 00:09:20.544
So congratulations,
governor, you played yourself

00:09:20.544 --> 00:09:23.764
you did the right thing
and immediately follow

00:09:23.764 --> 00:09:27.271
that up potentially allegedly
by doing the wrong thing.

00:09:27.271 --> 00:09:30.644
Now, what this means for his
political career is uncertain.

00:09:30.644 --> 00:09:32.888
New York attorney general Letitia James

00:09:32.888 --> 00:09:35.230
has appointed an independent
investigator to look

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:36.220
into these claims.

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:38.432
And many of the prominent
Democrats and Republicans

00:09:38.432 --> 00:09:40.807
in New York have called
on the governor to resign.

00:09:40.807 --> 00:09:44.229
The governor has often dismissed
this as cancel culture.

00:09:44.229 --> 00:09:46.455
But if indeed these allegations are true

00:09:46.455 --> 00:09:49.259
this isn't cancellation,
it's just consequences.

00:09:49.259 --> 00:09:50.937
Which takes us to the second scandal

00:09:50.937 --> 00:09:52.562
that Cuomo is facing related

00:09:52.562 --> 00:09:54.475
to nursing homes and COVID patients.

00:09:54.475 --> 00:09:57.424
Cuomo second scandals
started with a dispute

00:09:57.424 --> 00:09:59.990
over how the state of New
York handled COVID patients

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:03.827
in nursing homes between March
25th and May 10th in 2020.

00:10:03.827 --> 00:10:06.683
Early in the pandemic on March 25th

00:10:06.683 --> 00:10:09.400
Cuomo state health
department ordered nursing

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:12.440
homes to accept recovering COVID
19 patients from hospitals.

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:14.030
The directive said that

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:16.128
if a nursing home resident
had been hospitalized

00:10:16.128 --> 00:10:17.763
and was stable enough to be discharged

00:10:17.763 --> 00:10:20.678
the nursing facility
needed to readmit them.

00:10:20.678 --> 00:10:23.446
Cuomo rescinded the order on May 10th

00:10:23.446 --> 00:10:25.552
but what's the problem here?

00:10:25.552 --> 00:10:28.827
Well, on March 23rd, just
before Cuomo's March 25th order

00:10:28.827 --> 00:10:30.940
the federal government issued guidance

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:33.008
on how to handle COVID 19 in nursing homes

00:10:33.008 --> 00:10:35.394
not surprisingly the
guidance boil down to this

00:10:35.394 --> 00:10:37.530
a patient must be medically ready

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:39.420
for discharge to a
long-term care facility.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:41.005
And the facility must be capable

00:10:41.005 --> 00:10:43.397
of caring for patients
recovering from COVID 19.

00:10:43.397 --> 00:10:46.241
If either of those
conditions could not be met

00:10:46.241 --> 00:10:48.790
the facilities should not admit the person

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:50.092
because they could spread COVID-19

00:10:50.092 --> 00:10:51.523
throughout the entire nursing home.

00:10:51.523 --> 00:10:54.420
Now, the first area of
dispute here is whether

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.537
the state department of
health order conflicted with

00:10:56.537 --> 00:10:57.863
the federal government's guidance.

00:10:57.863 --> 00:10:59.316
Cuomo says it did not.

00:10:59.316 --> 00:11:00.740
The New York health

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:02.210
department's order requires a doctor

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:05.630
to confirm a resident is
medically stable for a return.

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:08.391
The order also says staff must conform

00:11:08.391 --> 00:11:09.944
to new York's requirements

00:11:09.944 --> 00:11:11.628
on environmental cleaning and PPE.

00:11:11.628 --> 00:11:14.314
On the other hand, it
clearly urges nursing

00:11:14.314 --> 00:11:16.370
homes to readmit COVID 19 patients,

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:18.997
"No resident shall be denied
readmission or admission

00:11:18.997 --> 00:11:22.957
"to the NH nursing home
solely based on a confirmed

00:11:22.957 --> 00:11:25.017
"or suspected diagnosis of COVID-19."

00:11:25.017 --> 00:11:26.836
NHs are prohibited

00:11:26.836 --> 00:11:29.798
from requiring a hospitalized
resident who is determined

00:11:29.798 --> 00:11:31.168
medically stable to be tested

00:11:31.168 --> 00:11:34.373
for COVID-19 prior to
admission or readmission.

00:11:34.373 --> 00:11:36.464
That's pretty strong language.

00:11:36.464 --> 00:11:39.370
And it precluded nursing
homes from doing testing

00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:42.048
as a basis for denying
a patient readmission.

00:11:42.048 --> 00:11:43.820
New York's logic was

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:45.629
that removing recovering
patients would free

00:11:45.629 --> 00:11:46.735
up hospital capacity

00:11:46.735 --> 00:11:49.610
which was thought to
be in dwindling supply.

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:51.985
And at one point was at
almost full capacity.

00:11:51.985 --> 00:11:53.656
However, we now know

00:11:53.656 --> 00:11:55.998
that this could have
been a dangerous move.

00:11:55.998 --> 00:11:58.702
Recovering patients still shed the virus

00:11:58.702 --> 00:11:59.901
creating a strong possibility

00:11:59.901 --> 00:12:01.529
that they could infect other people

00:12:01.529 --> 00:12:04.128
in the nursing homes if they
were not properly quarantine.

00:12:04.128 --> 00:12:06.690
And the people in nursing homes tend to be

00:12:06.690 --> 00:12:09.696
over the age of 70, the
very age group that is most

00:12:09.696 --> 00:12:11.618
at risk of death from COVID-19.

00:12:11.618 --> 00:12:14.232
Cuomo claims that he followed

00:12:14.232 --> 00:12:16.936
the Trump administration's
guidelines on nursing homes.

00:12:16.936 --> 00:12:20.292
But after the March 25th order,
there was immediate pushback

00:12:20.292 --> 00:12:23.608
by various advocacy groups
who felt that Cuomo was

00:12:23.608 --> 00:12:27.351
mandating the nursing homes
admit COVID 19 positive people

00:12:27.351 --> 00:12:30.139
regardless of whether they
could meet safety standards.

00:12:30.139 --> 00:12:32.473
Cuomo himself, publicly
pressured facilities

00:12:32.473 --> 00:12:34.397
to find space for COVID patients.

00:12:34.397 --> 00:12:36.034
- They don't have the right to object.

00:12:36.034 --> 00:12:37.885
- He said on April 23rd.

00:12:37.885 --> 00:12:40.044
And yet Cuomo's further comments

00:12:40.044 --> 00:12:42.952
on that day actually did
track the federal guidelines.

00:12:42.952 --> 00:12:44.913
- So if you can't provide adequate care

00:12:44.913 --> 00:12:46.689
you can't have the patient

00:12:46.689 --> 00:12:50.975
in your facility and that's
your basic fiduciary obligation.

00:12:50.975 --> 00:12:52.608
I would say ethical obligation.

00:12:52.608 --> 00:12:54.901
And it's also your legal obligation.

00:12:54.901 --> 00:12:57.115
If you can't provide adequate care

00:12:57.115 --> 00:12:59.397
the person must be transferred.

00:12:59.397 --> 00:13:04.281
If you have COVID people,
they have to be quarantined.

00:13:04.281 --> 00:13:06.353
They have to have separate staff.

00:13:06.353 --> 00:13:07.957
That's the rule.

00:13:07.957 --> 00:13:09.818
If you can't do it

00:13:09.818 --> 00:13:12.180
we'll put them in a
facility that can do it.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:13.299
That's the rule

00:13:13.299 --> 00:13:15.990
- That's the underlying
issue, but there's also

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:18.070
an undercounting and
potential under-reporting

00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:21.145
issue regarding the data
related to nursing home deaths.

00:13:21.145 --> 00:13:23.582
Now the second scandal
also involved how the state

00:13:23.582 --> 00:13:25.865
calculated and reported
nursing home deaths.

00:13:25.865 --> 00:13:28.610
In particular when a nursing home resident

00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:30.107
who contracted COVID 19 died

00:13:30.107 --> 00:13:32.416
after being transported to
a hospital for treatment.

00:13:32.416 --> 00:13:34.554
The person wasn't included as,

00:13:34.554 --> 00:13:36.476
"Nursing home death."

00:13:36.476 --> 00:13:39.690
In New York, this wasn't
immediately apparent

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:42.340
from the numbers and
explanation the state officials

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:43.173
were releasing to the press.

00:13:43.173 --> 00:13:46.260
Eventually by May though
the state confirmed

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:48.792
that it did not include
these deaths in its count.

00:13:48.792 --> 00:13:50.220
And while the legislature

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:51.806
and media were asking
about the correct numbers

00:13:51.806 --> 00:13:54.346
Cuomo was enjoying great
national popularity

00:13:54.346 --> 00:13:56.678
for his televised COVID-19 briefings

00:13:56.678 --> 00:13:59.900
and promoting his book American
crisis leadership lessons

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:00.770
from the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:14:00.770 --> 00:14:02.774
And Cuomo was well on his way

00:14:02.774 --> 00:14:04.321
to America's governor status.

00:14:04.321 --> 00:14:05.433
Just like Rudy Giuliani

00:14:05.433 --> 00:14:07.473
had once been considered America's mayor.

00:14:07.473 --> 00:14:10.259
And like Rudy Cuomo had
high approval ratings

00:14:10.259 --> 00:14:11.943
that led many to speculate

00:14:11.943 --> 00:14:13.685
that he would one day become president.

00:14:13.685 --> 00:14:15.659
And what happened next?

00:14:15.659 --> 00:14:19.330
Well, as Rudy knows pride
go with before the fall.

00:14:19.330 --> 00:14:21.697
By fall Trump's justice
department was on the case.

00:14:21.697 --> 00:14:25.790
And assistant AG sent Cuomo
a request for complete data

00:14:25.790 --> 00:14:27.997
on all public and private
nursing home facilities.

00:14:27.997 --> 00:14:29.780
Cuomo claimed he was being harassed

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:30.905
by the Trump administration

00:14:30.905 --> 00:14:33.388
but it wasn't just the
Trump justice department.

00:14:33.388 --> 00:14:35.952
A patient advocacy group
did a foyer request

00:14:35.952 --> 00:14:37.075
for nursing home data.

00:14:37.075 --> 00:14:39.138
And of course he was still tangling

00:14:39.138 --> 00:14:41.101
with the legislature over the numbers.

00:14:41.101 --> 00:14:44.274
And at the same time, Cuomo
tried to Stonewall everyone.

00:14:44.274 --> 00:14:47.140
But in January Cuomo's
own attorney general

00:14:47.140 --> 00:14:49.733
Letitia James issued a
scathing report on the matter,

00:14:49.733 --> 00:14:52.469
"This preliminary for the 62 facilities.

00:14:52.469 --> 00:14:54.707
"And the time periods noted above suggest

00:14:54.707 --> 00:14:56.677
"the COVID-19 resident deaths associated

00:14:56.677 --> 00:14:59.097
"with nursing homes in New
York state appear to be

00:14:59.097 --> 00:15:01.997
"under counted by DOH
by approximately 50%."

00:15:01.997 --> 00:15:04.820
The health department
quickly revised the death

00:15:04.820 --> 00:15:08.117
report showing an additional 3,829 deaths

00:15:08.117 --> 00:15:09.968
of nursing home residents.

00:15:09.968 --> 00:15:12.356
Governor Cuomo and
Michigan governor Whitmer

00:15:12.356 --> 00:15:14.250
issued a joint statement complaining

00:15:14.250 --> 00:15:15.770
that they were facing scrutiny

00:15:15.770 --> 00:15:16.691
from a Trump administration.

00:15:16.691 --> 00:15:18.097
That was hell bent on revenge.

00:15:18.097 --> 00:15:20.660
The former president apparently hated both

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:22.555
of these public officials
who clashed with him often.

00:15:22.555 --> 00:15:23.680
But here's the thing,

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:26.217
"You can't complain that
there's a political witch hunt

00:15:26.217 --> 00:15:27.872
"if you actually did something wrong."

00:15:27.872 --> 00:15:30.010
And it's probably fair
to say that it should not

00:15:30.010 --> 00:15:31.650
have taken nine months for
the state to be upfront

00:15:31.650 --> 00:15:32.954
about these particular numbers.

00:15:32.954 --> 00:15:34.921
Based on the facts that we have now,

00:15:34.921 --> 00:15:37.578
it looks like not all of Cuomo's
actions were on the level.

00:15:37.578 --> 00:15:38.840
But the question is

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:41.010
did governor Cuomo violate any law?

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:43.470
Obviously governors receive
wide latitude in terms

00:15:43.470 --> 00:15:45.167
of the policy decisions
that they have to make.

00:15:45.167 --> 00:15:47.870
Well, when the Trump justice
department sought data

00:15:47.870 --> 00:15:50.022
on the nursing home
matters, it's cited as power

00:15:50.022 --> 00:15:52.829
under the civil rights of
institutionalized persons act.

00:15:52.829 --> 00:15:56.380
The CRIPA authorizes
the U S attorney general

00:15:56.380 --> 00:15:58.111
to investigate conditions of confinement

00:15:58.111 --> 00:15:59.889
at state and local government institutions

00:15:59.889 --> 00:16:02.943
such as prisons, jail,
juvenile correction facilities

00:16:02.943 --> 00:16:05.031
and publicly operated nursing homes.

00:16:05.031 --> 00:16:07.608
Now CRIPA only applies
to public facilities

00:16:07.608 --> 00:16:09.320
which only account for about 5%

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:10.338
of the state's nursing homes.

00:16:10.338 --> 00:16:13.168
But the attorney general
can investigate widespread

00:16:13.168 --> 00:16:15.772
deficiencies that seriously
jeopardize the health

00:16:15.772 --> 00:16:17.659
and safety of public
nursing home residents.

00:16:17.659 --> 00:16:20.160
However, the government
does not have authority

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:21.905
under CRIPA to investigate
isolated incidents

00:16:21.905 --> 00:16:23.977
or to represent individuals.

00:16:23.977 --> 00:16:26.235
For the attorney general to sue.

00:16:26.235 --> 00:16:28.430
There has to be a
reasonable cause to believe

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:31.536
that the nursing home conditions
are egregious or flagrant

00:16:31.536 --> 00:16:33.414
that they are subjecting residents to,

00:16:33.414 --> 00:16:34.299
"Grievous harm."

00:16:34.299 --> 00:16:36.277
And that they are part of a,

00:16:36.277 --> 00:16:38.207
"Pattern or practice of resistance

00:16:38.207 --> 00:16:41.580
"to a resident's constitutional
or federal rights."

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:43.340
So the CRIPA covers things

00:16:43.340 --> 00:16:45.518
like a pattern of sexual abuse, neglect

00:16:45.518 --> 00:16:48.138
unmet mental health needs,
or inadequate education

00:16:48.138 --> 00:16:49.630
and facilities that host children.

00:16:49.630 --> 00:16:50.563
Was the situation

00:16:50.563 --> 00:16:52.907
in new York's public
nursing homes, abusive

00:16:52.907 --> 00:16:53.983
or grossly neglectful?

00:16:53.983 --> 00:16:55.993
That has yet to be determined.

00:16:55.993 --> 00:16:58.941
But in July New York health
authorities presented data

00:16:58.941 --> 00:17:00.860
allegedly showing that the Corona virus

00:17:00.860 --> 00:17:03.290
had infected nursing homes far earlier

00:17:03.290 --> 00:17:04.212
than once believed.

00:17:04.212 --> 00:17:05.045
In that,

00:17:05.045 --> 00:17:07.459
"Readmitting residents
that had been hospitalized

00:17:07.459 --> 00:17:10.077
"for coronavirus did not contribute

00:17:10.077 --> 00:17:11.794
"to a higher fatality
rate in those facilities."

00:17:11.794 --> 00:17:14.609
But that's just one opinion given

00:17:14.609 --> 00:17:16.477
by Cuomo's own health authorities

00:17:16.477 --> 00:17:19.245
during the same month that
he signed his book deal.

00:17:19.245 --> 00:17:21.917
The report compiled by attorney general

00:17:21.917 --> 00:17:24.023
James saw things differently.

00:17:24.023 --> 00:17:24.856
James found,

00:17:24.856 --> 00:17:27.400
"Lack of compliance with
infection control protocols

00:17:27.400 --> 00:17:29.947
"insufficient personal
protective equipment PPE

00:17:29.947 --> 00:17:33.308
"for nursing home staff
insufficient COVID-19 testing

00:17:33.308 --> 00:17:35.927
"for residents and staff
in the early stages

00:17:35.927 --> 00:17:38.347
"of the pandemic and the
state reimbursement model

00:17:38.347 --> 00:17:40.047
"for nursing homes that gave,

00:17:40.047 --> 00:17:41.567
"Financial incentive to owners

00:17:41.567 --> 00:17:43.970
"of for-profit nursing
homes to transfer funds

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:45.236
"to related parties

00:17:45.236 --> 00:17:48.314
"ultimately increasing their
own profit instead of investing

00:17:48.314 --> 00:17:50.396
"in higher levels of staffing and PPE."

00:17:50.396 --> 00:17:52.840
Cuomo claims that other States issued

00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:53.963
similar nursing home directives

00:17:53.963 --> 00:17:55.576
and are not being investigated.

00:17:55.576 --> 00:17:57.720
However, once again, that
doesn't tell us anything

00:17:57.720 --> 00:17:59.500
about whether what New York did would be

00:17:59.500 --> 00:18:01.878
considered widespread abuse
or neglect under the law.

00:18:01.878 --> 00:18:04.831
Although Cuomo hasn't resigned,
he has lost the support

00:18:04.831 --> 00:18:06.278
of the democratic party in the state

00:18:06.278 --> 00:18:08.809
which controls both houses
of the state government.

00:18:08.809 --> 00:18:11.056
And the Senate judiciary
committee has opened

00:18:11.056 --> 00:18:12.223
an impeachment inquiry.

00:18:12.223 --> 00:18:13.640
Now that's a lot of scandals

00:18:13.640 --> 00:18:15.290
for one governor and
it's only been possible

00:18:15.290 --> 00:18:17.067
because whistleblowers have come forward.

